import { caseQuestions } from "./cases";

export const architectAssessment = [
  ...caseQuestions("Customer Support Resolution Agent", "You are building a customer support resolution agent with the Claude Agent SDK for a subscription retailer. It reaches backend systems through custom MCP tools: get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund and escalate_to_human. Refunds above $250 need a supervisor approval tied to the order. The target is 80% first-contact resolution, and the same refund service is also called by the human support desk.", [
    ["rail-loop", "1.1", "stop", "A trace shows one assistant response with `stop_reason: \"tool_use\"`: a short text preamble, then two tool_use blocks, toolu_17 calling get_customer and toolu_21 calling lookup_order. The MCP adapter returns toolu_21's result first, 300 ms before toolu_17 finishes. The harness currently sends only the first result that arrives, and the next call fails with a 400 error. What should the next Messages API request contain?", "c", "When a response stops on tool_use, every tool_use block must be answered in the next user message with a tool_result carrying its tool_use_id. Parallel calls can finish in any order because the IDs, not position or arrival time, link each result to its call.",
      ["The assistant message unchanged, then a user message whose text quotes both tool outputs in the order they finished.", "Quoted text is not a tool_result, so both tool_use blocks remain unanswered and the API rejects the request just as it did with one missing result."],
      ["The assistant message unchanged, then a user message with one tool_result for toolu_21 that bundles both outputs together.", "Each tool_use ID needs its own tool_result. Bundling toolu_17's output under toolu_21 leaves toolu_17 unanswered and mislabels which tool produced which data."],
      ["The assistant message unchanged, then one user message holding a tool_result block for each tool_use_id, paired by ID.", "Every call is answered by ID in the immediately following user message, so completion order does not matter and the 400 error disappears."],
      ["The assistant message unchanged, then a user message with toolu_21's result, sending toolu_17's result in a later turn.", "Results for all tool_use blocks in a response belong in the next user message; deferring toolu_17 repeats the same 400 error on this request."]],
    ["rail-loop-ended", "1.1", "stop", "The agent's loop exits when a response contains no tool_use blocks. Logs from 2,000 sessions show 31 replies truncated mid-sentence; each of those responses has `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` and `usage.output_tokens` equal to the configured 1,024 limit. Separately, 4 sessions end with `stop_reason: \"pause_turn\"` after a server-side web search tool ran. Which loop change handles both cases correctly?", "a", "Loop control should read the structured stop_reason field. tool_use means run tools and continue, end_turn means finished, max_tokens means the output was truncated, and pause_turn means a server-tool loop paused and the assistant content should be sent back. Text cues and turn caps are not exit conditions.",
      ["Branch on stop_reason: end on end_turn, continue past max_tokens, and send the assistant content back on pause_turn.", "Each value has its own handling; max_tokens means the output was cut off, and pause_turn means the server tool loop paused and can resume."],
      ["Keep exiting when no tool_use block appears, but raise max_tokens to 4,096 so fewer replies hit the output limit.", "A larger limit makes truncation rarer without fixing the exit test, and the pause_turn sessions still end early with an unfinished answer."],
      ["Exit when the final text block contains a closing phrase such as 'anything else', and otherwise call the API again.", "Parsing natural-language wording is unreliable; phrasing varies, and a truncated or paused reply may contain or omit the phrase by chance."],
      ["Cap the loop at ten iterations with max_turns and treat whatever response arrives last as the final customer answer.", "An iteration cap is a safety net, not a completion signal; truncated and paused replies would still be delivered as finished answers."]],
    ["rail-approval", "1.4", "agents", "A supervisor approved a $180 refund on order 4471 at 10:02. At 10:09 the customer asks for $280 instead, and the agent calls process_refund with amount 280 while the 10:02 approval is still in the conversation. The human desk calls the same refund service directly. An audit finds 6 of 900 refunds last month exceeded their approved amount. Where should the approval rule be enforced?", "d", "Put an invariant at the narrowest point every caller must pass through. When an agent and humans share one service, a hook or prompt in the agent harness protects only one path; validating the approval against order, amount and actor inside the service blocks violations deterministically before the side effect.",
      ["Add a system prompt rule that any change to the refund amount requires the agent to request a fresh supervisor approval.", "A prompt rule is probabilistic and only covers the agent; the desk's direct calls to the refund service would still bypass the check."],
      ["Add a PreToolUse hook on process_refund in the agent harness that denies calls whose amount exceeds the approved figure.", "The hook stops the agent's mismatched calls, but the human desk calls the service directly, and the 6 audited overages could come from that path."],
      ["Run a nightly reconciliation job that flags refunds above their approved amount and routes them to finance for reversal.", "Reconciliation detects the overage after money has moved; the requirement is to prevent an unapproved refund, not to reverse it later."],
      ["Have the refund service reject any write unless a stored approval matches the caller, order and exact amount.", "Both callers pass through the shared service, so checking the approval binding there prevents the $280 refund on either path before it executes."]],
    ["rail-hooks", "1.5", "hooks", "Two requirements arrive for the agent. First, process_refund must not run for an order whose lookup_order result shows `chargeback_open: true`. Second, lookup_order returns `created` as Unix seconds from one backend and as `03/04/2026` text from another, and 14% of date answers last week were wrong. Which two hook configurations meet these requirements?", "bc", "Choose the hook event by when the data exists and whether the action must be prevented. PreToolUse sees the requested input and can deny before execution; PostToolUse sees the actual result and can normalize it before Claude reads it, but it cannot undo an action already taken.",
      ["A PostToolUse hook on process_refund that detects chargeback orders and issues a reversing refund call afterward.", "PostToolUse runs after the refund executes, so the money has already moved; a reversing call is a compensating action, not prevention."],
      ["A PreToolUse hook on process_refund that returns permissionDecision deny when the recorded order shows an open chargeback.", "PreToolUse fires before execution and can deny the call with a reason, which is the deterministic block the first requirement needs."],
      ["A PostToolUse hook on lookup_order that rewrites `created` to ISO 8601 through updatedToolOutput before Claude reads it.", "Normalizing the returned value after the tool runs and before the model reasons gives one consistent date format from both backends."],
      ["A PreToolUse hook on lookup_order that converts the `created` field to ISO 8601 before the lookup executes.", "The `created` field does not exist until the lookup returns, so a PreToolUse hook has nothing to convert at that point."],
      ["A SessionStart hook that loads a system prompt rule telling Claude to treat chargeback orders as non-refundable.", "Loading a prompt rule at session start is still probabilistic guidance, so some chargeback refunds would continue to execute."]],
    ["rail-workload", "1.2", "agents", "For a billing dispute, the coordinator delegates to two subagents. The order-history subagent returns all 12 invoices for account C-88. The policy subagent returns two applicable clauses and reports `coverage: partial — regional late-fee policy not found in index`. That regional policy decides whether a $64 late fee is refundable. The coordinator then drafts a final answer citing only the two clauses. What should the coordinator do instead?", "b", "A coordinator should read each subagent's coverage report before synthesis. Keep verified results, re-delegate only the specific missing scope that affects the decision, and avoid both restarting completed work and filling a reported gap with a substitute source.",
      ["Re-run both subagents with a broader shared prompt so each one searches invoices and policies together.", "The invoice work is complete; repeating it wastes time, and merging scopes blurs which subagent owns the missing regional policy."],
      ["Keep the 12 invoices and two clauses, and delegate a targeted lookup for the regional late-fee policy.", "This keeps verified results and assigns exactly the gap the policy subagent reported, which is the fact the $64 decision depends on."],
      ["Have the synthesis step apply the general late-fee clause in place of the missing regional policy for this account.", "Substituting a general clause for a reported gap answers a different question and may give the customer the wrong refund decision."],
      ["Send the draft answer with a note that policy coverage was partial and let the customer escalate if needed.", "Disclosing the gap beats hiding it, but the deciding policy is retrievable now, so pushing the gap to the customer lowers first-contact resolution."]],
    ["rail-session", "1.7", "sessions", "An engineer debugging refund escalations has a Claude Code session named `refund-baseline` in which 40 minutes of log analysis established the failure pattern. She wants to compare two prompt fixes, each starting from that analysis, without either experiment's turns appearing in the other or in `refund-baseline`. Which commands should she run?", "c", "Resume continues an existing session and appends to it; forking creates a new session ID from a copy of the history. To compare alternatives from one verified baseline, fork once per alternative with `--resume <name> --fork-session` (or `fork_session` in the SDK) so the baseline and each branch stay isolated.",
      ["Run `claude --resume refund-baseline` twice, trying one prompt fix in each resumed session before comparing results.", "Resuming without forking appends both experiments to the same refund-baseline history, so the second fix inherits the first fix's turns."],
      ["Run `claude --continue` for the first fix, then `claude --continue --fork-session` for the second fix.", "--continue opens the most recent conversation in the directory, which may not be refund-baseline, and the first run still writes into it."],
      ["Run `claude --resume refund-baseline --fork-session` once per fix, giving each experiment its own new session ID.", "Forking on resume copies the baseline history into a new session ID each time, so experiments stay independent and the baseline is untouched."],
      ["Run `/compact` inside refund-baseline, then start two fresh sessions and paste the compacted summary into each one.", "A pasted summary loses the detailed tool results behind the analysis, and /compact also rewrites the baseline's own context."]],
    ["rail-tool-boundary", "2.1", "tool", "Production logs show that for 'has my replacement shipped?' messages, the agent calls `update_order` 9% of the time instead of `lookup_order`; three calls this week changed a delivery address. Both tool descriptions read 'Handles order information' and both accept `order_id`. Inquiry-only sessions authenticated as `role: viewer` also see `update_order`. What is the most effective first change?", "a", "Tool descriptions are the main signal Claude uses to choose between tools. When near-identical descriptions cause misselection, first make each description state what it does, its inputs and when to use the other tool, and expose write tools only to roles that need them, before adding routers or examples.",
      ["Rewrite both descriptions to state reads versus writes, inputs and when to use each, and omit update_order for viewers.", "Minimal, identical descriptions are the root cause of the selection errors, and removing the write tool from viewer sessions closes the access path."],
      ["Add eight few-shot examples to the system prompt showing shipping questions routed to lookup_order instead of update_order.", "Examples may lower the error rate, but the descriptions stay identical and viewer sessions can still invoke the write tool."],
      ["Add a keyword router that inspects each message and preselects lookup_order whenever it contains the word 'shipped'.", "A keyword router is a disproportionate first step that bypasses model judgment and misroutes requests such as 'change where it shipped'."],
      ["Merge both tools into one manage_order tool with an action parameter that the model sets to read or write.", "Consolidation still exposes write capability to viewers and moves the same ambiguity into an action parameter with no clearer description."]],
    ["rail-empty-timeout", "2.2", "mcp", "Logs show the agent retried process_refund three times on each of these results: R1 `isError: true, errorCategory: \"validation\", message: \"order_id must match ORD-#####\"`; R2 `isError: true, errorCategory: \"business\", isRetryable: false, message: \"refund window closed after 30 days\"`; R3 `isError: true, errorCategory: \"transient\", isRetryable: true` after a gateway 503. Which handling should the agent apply?", "c", "Structured tool errors are only useful if the agent branches on them. Validation errors need corrected input, business-rule errors need an explanation and an allowed alternative, and transient errors can be retried within budget. Uniform retries or uniform escalation ignore the category the tool already provided.",
      ["Retry all three with exponential backoff up to five attempts, then escalate any that still fail to a human.", "Backoff suits only R3; repeating R1's malformed ID or R2's closed window fails identically every time and delays the customer."],
      ["Treat the three as tool failures, apologize that refunds are unavailable, and call escalate_to_human for each order.", "R1 is fixable by asking for the correct ID and R2 has a clear policy answer, so escalating everything lowers first-contact resolution."],
      ["Ask for a corrected order ID on R1, explain the 30-day window on R2, and retry R3 once.", "Each category maps to its own recovery: fix the input, explain the business rule without retrying, and retry only the transient gateway failure."],
      ["Have the PostToolUse hook rewrite the three results to `isError: false` with a polite message so the conversation continues.", "Masking errors as success hides why the refund failed, so the agent can no longer correct the input or retry the transient failure."]],
    ["rail-tool-retry", "2.2", "mcp", "Two calls time out in one conversation. A `lookup_order` read timed out after 900 ms; the turn still has 1.4 s of its 3 s budget and the tool allows one retry. A `process_refund` call for $120 timed out after the gateway received it; the tool sends no idempotency key, but `get_refund_status(order_id)` can show whether it posted. What should the agent do?", "b", "Retry policy depends on whether an operation is idempotent. Reads can be retried within the remaining deadline. A write that timed out may have committed, so reconcile its state through a read-only status check before repeating it; a fresh request ID makes a duplicate more likely, not less.",
      ["Retry both calls once within the remaining budget and reverse any duplicate refund the finance team finds later.", "A retry is safe for the read, but repeating a write that may have posted risks a duplicate $120 refund that only gets caught afterward."],
      ["Retry the lookup once now, and check get_refund_status before any second refund attempt is made.", "The read retry fits the 1.4 s budget, and the status check resolves whether the timed-out refund committed before anything is repeated."],
      ["Retry the lookup once, and resend the refund with a newly generated request ID so the gateway treats it separately.", "A fresh request ID guarantees the gateway processes the second refund as new, which is exactly the duplicate the status check would prevent."],
      ["Skip the lookup retry to save latency, and tell the customer the $120 refund failed so they can request it again.", "The budget allows the read retry, and declaring the refund failed is unverified; the customer could receive two refunds."]],
    ["rail-role", "2.3", "tool", "A triage subagent needs only order status for the verified customer on the current ticket, but it was configured with the full 11-tool support toolset; last week it called process_refund twice while summarizing tickets. The order service accepts any `order_id` from any authenticated caller. Which two changes create a meaningful authority boundary?", "ad", "Least privilege needs two layers: limit which tools an agent can see, and have the backend authorize each call's scope. Prompt prohibitions, pre-approval lists and after-the-fact audits change behavior or detection, but none of them removes the capability or validates the record at execution time.",
      ["Set the triage AgentDefinition `tools` to a status-only `get_order_status` tool instead of the full toolset.", "Restricting the subagent's tools list removes process_refund from its options entirely, rather than asking it to refrain from using it."],
      ["Add a line to the triage prompt saying it must not call process_refund or any other write tool.", "Prompt instructions reduce but do not prevent misuse; the subagent still holds process_refund and already called it twice."],
      ["List `process_refund` in the triage subagent's `allowedTools` with a comment that it is reserved for emergencies.", "allowedTools pre-approves tools so they run without a permission prompt, which widens rather than narrows the subagent's authority."],
      ["Have the order service check that each requested `order_id` belongs to the verified customer on the ticket.", "Server-side scoping stops cross-customer reads even if the subagent passes a wrong ID, enforcing the record boundary at execution."],
      ["Log every triage tool call to an audit table and review refunds issued by triage at the end of each week.", "Weekly review detects misuse after refunds are issued; it is a detective control, not a boundary that prevents the call."]],
    ["rail-team-mcp", "2.4", "mcpConfig", "The support team's order-history MCP server works only on the lead's laptop, where `claude mcp add` stored it with the default scope. New hires cloning the repository see no `mcp__orders__*` tools, and the server's token rotates every 30 days per person. Where should the server definition live so every clone gets it without committing a token?", "d", "Claude Code MCP scopes decide who gets a server. Local scope, the default for `claude mcp add`, and user scope live in `~/.claude.json` and are never shared. A team server belongs in project-scope `.mcp.json`, with secrets referenced through `${VAR}` so each developer or CI runner supplies its own.",
      ["In each developer's `~/.claude.json`, added with `claude mcp add --scope user` and the token pasted separately per machine.", "User scope follows one person across projects but is not in the repository, so each new hire still has to configure it by hand."],
      ["In a committed `.claude/config.json` file with an `mcpServers` array and the token read from a shared vault.", "Claude Code does not read MCP servers from `.claude/config.json`; that configuration location does not exist, so clones still see no tools."],
      ["In the root CLAUDE.md, with the server URL and setup steps so Claude can register the server on first use.", "CLAUDE.md supplies instructions, not MCP configuration; Claude cannot register a server from prose, so new clones still see no tools."],
      ["In the repository's `.mcp.json` at project scope, with the token written as `${ORDERS_TOKEN}` from each environment.", "Project-scope `.mcp.json` is committed and shared by every clone, and `${VAR}` expansion keeps each person's rotating token out of git."]],
    ["rail-resume-exact", "5.1", "context", "A customer has two open orders: ORD-10442, promised for 12 March, and ORD-10467, promised for 19 March. After automatic compaction at 160,000 tokens, the agent tells the customer both orders arrive on 19 March, and a $40 late-delivery credit is applied to the wrong order. What should the agent carry across compaction to prevent this?", "a", "Exact transactional facts such as IDs, amounts and dates should live in durable structured state, not only in conversation history that compaction summarizes. Re-inject that keyed record on each turn; larger windows or better summarizer wording reduce the chance of loss but do not preserve the associations.",
      ["A case-facts block, kept outside the compacted history, that lists each order ID with its promised date and source.", "Structured per-order facts survive compaction intact and are re-injected each turn, so dates and the credit stay attached to the right order."],
      ["A larger compaction threshold of 400,000 tokens so the original order details remain in the context for longer.", "A later compaction delays the problem without changing how summaries merge similar facts, so the dates can still collapse once it triggers."],
      ["A custom compaction instruction asking the summarizer to preserve dates and order numbers exactly when it summarizes.", "Better wording may help, but the summary remains prose generated by the model, so exact order-to-date links are still not guaranteed."],
      ["The most recent five turns kept verbatim plus the summary, since the customer mentioned both dates recently.", "Recent turns may not contain both promises, and the summary that merged the dates is still the only source for older facts."]],
    ["rail-handoff", "5.2", "agents", "Two conversations reach turn 6. Customer A writes 'I want to talk to a person' after two failed process_refund attempts on a damaged item. Customer B asks for a refund, but get_customer returns three accounts for 'J. Patel'; B has an order number from the receipt email. The team's first-contact resolution is 61%. Which dispositions are correct?", "c", "Escalate immediately when a customer explicitly asks for a human, passing a structured summary so they do not repeat themselves. When a lookup returns several matches, ask for a distinguishing identifier rather than choosing by heuristics; escalation is for unresolvable or out-of-policy cases, not for ambiguity one question can fix.",
      ["Make one more automated refund attempt for A, and escalate B to a human because the account match is ambiguous.", "A explicitly asked for a person, and B's ambiguity is resolvable with the order number, so this reverses both correct dispositions."],
      ["Escalate both to humans with full transcripts, since two failures and an ambiguous match both signal elevated risk.", "B can be resolved in one clarifying question, so escalating it lowers first-contact resolution without reducing any real risk."],
      ["Call escalate_to_human for A with the attempts summarized, and ask B for the order number before any refund.", "This honors A's explicit request with context for staff and resolves B's three-way match with one identifier instead of guessing."],
      ["Select the most recently active J. Patel account for B, and offer A a store credit before deciding on escalation.", "Recency is a guess that can refund the wrong account, and delaying A's explicit request for a person ignores a clear escalation trigger."]],
    ["rail-attempts", "5.3", "context", "A shipment-tracking subagent checks 5 carrier legs for a lost package. Legs 1–3 return verified scans; legs 4–5 fail after its single allowed retry with `carrier_api: 504`. The coordinator received `{\"status\": \"error\"}` and nothing else, so it told the customer tracking is unavailable. What should the subagent return instead?", "d", "Subagent failures should come back as structured context: the failure type, what was attempted, any partial results and possible alternatives. That lets the coordinator recover intelligently. Bare error codes, fail-silent success and workflow-ending exceptions each remove information the coordinator needs.",
      ["A success status listing the three verified scans, with legs 4 and 5 omitted because no scan data came back.", "Omitting failed legs as if they had no scans marks a failure as success, so the coordinator would report incomplete tracking as complete."],
      ["The same error status after exponential backoff retries up to ten times, so transient carrier outages have time to recover.", "Extra retries exceed the one-retry budget and still return a bare status, which hides the three verified legs from the coordinator."],
      ["An exception that propagates to the top-level handler and ends the conversation with a generic apology message.", "Terminating the workflow discards three verified scans that could partly answer the customer's question right now."],
      ["The three verified scans, the two failed legs with error 504, the retry already attempted, and suggested next steps.", "Structured partial results let the coordinator answer with what is known and decide whether to try another source for legs 4–5."]],
    ["rail-proof", "5.6", "grounding", "For a goodwill-credit decision, the knowledge subagent returns 'average delivery delay: 4.2 days (carrier dashboard, Q3, all regions)', and the order subagent returns 'delay for ORD-20931: 9 days (warehouse scan log, shipped 2 Oct)'. The draft reply says 'your delay of about 4 days is within our average'. How should the agent ground the reply?", "b", "Keep claims attached to their source, scope and period through synthesis. Figures that look contradictory often measure different populations or windows. Report each with its provenance and explain the relationship, instead of averaging, picking the larger source, or dropping specifics the decision needs.",
      ["Average the two figures to 6.6 days and present that as the customer's expected delay in the reply.", "The figures measure different things, a regional average and one order's delay, so averaging them produces a number that describes neither."],
      ["State this order's 9-day delay from the scan log, and cite the 4.2-day Q3 average separately as context.", "Each claim keeps its own source, scope and period, so the customer sees their actual delay and how it compares with the average."],
      ["Use the 4.2-day figure because the carrier dashboard covers more shipments and is the more authoritative source.", "A larger sample does not make an aggregate the correct value for one order; the scan log directly measures this customer's delay."],
      ["Remove both numbers and say the delay was longer than usual, so the reply avoids citing conflicting data.", "The figures do not conflict once scope is attached, and dropping them withholds the specific evidence the credit decision depends on."]],
  ]),
  ...caseQuestions("Multi-Agent Research System", "You are building a multi-agent research system with the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent delegates through the Agent tool (formerly Task) to a web search subagent, a document analysis subagent, a synthesis subagent and a report subagent. Subagents run in isolated contexts with their own tool lists. Reports must cite a source for every claim and state any coverage gaps before publication.", [
    ["water-plan", "1.2", "research", "Asked to research 'causes of supply-chain delays in 2025', the system returns well-cited reports that cover only ocean freight. Every subagent reports success. The coordinator log shows `subtasks: [\"container port congestion\", \"shipping lane disruptions\", \"ocean freight rates\"]`. Reviewers note that trucking, rail, customs and labor causes are absent from all 14 reports. What is the most likely root cause?", "c", "When every subagent succeeds yet the output misses whole areas, inspect the coordinator's decomposition first. Subagents can only cover the scope they are assigned. Fixing downstream prompts treats symptoms; the production fix changes how the coordinator partitions the topic and checks coverage before synthesis.",
      ["The web search subagent's queries are too narrow and should be expanded to include land transport and customs terms.", "The search subagent searched exactly the three subtasks it was assigned; widening its queries treats a symptom downstream of the decomposition."],
      ["The synthesis subagent lacks an instruction to identify coverage gaps before passing findings to the report subagent.", "A gap check would help catch the problem, but synthesis faithfully combined what it received; the missing areas were never assigned."],
      ["The coordinator's decomposition is too narrow, so the subtasks it assigned never cover the topic's other causes.", "The log shows all three subtasks are ocean-freight topics, so subagents succeeded at an incomplete assignment; the coverage loss starts at decomposition."],
      ["The document analysis subagent's relevance filter is discarding sources about trucking, rail, customs and labor.", "No evidence shows such sources were retrieved and filtered; the subtasks never requested them, so blaming analysis targets the wrong component."]],
    ["water-packets", "1.3", "subagents", "A headless run of the coordinator stalls; its log ends with `Permission required: Agent`, and `allowedTools` lists only `[\"Read\", \"WebSearch\"]`. In an earlier interactive run, the document analysis subagent returned findings for all 9 regions; its prompt read 'analyze the region we discussed', while the brief named region R-4 and the 2024–2026 window. Which two changes fix these failures?", "be", "A coordinator can spawn subagents only if the Agent tool (formerly Task) is in its allowedTools, and each subagent starts with nothing but its own prompt and tools. Pass every scope fact explicitly; do not rely on shared history, longer turn limits or session forking to supply context.",
      ["Set `fork_session: true` on the subagent call so it inherits the coordinator's conversation and knows which region was discussed.", "fork_session branches a resumed session; subagents start from their own prompt and do not inherit the coordinator's history this way."],
      ["Add `\"Agent\"` to the coordinator's `allowedTools` so it can spawn subagents without waiting for a permission prompt.", "The coordinator invokes subagents through the Agent tool (Task in the exam guide); without pre-approval, a headless run blocks on the permission request."],
      ["Add `\"Agent\"` to the document analysis subagent's `disallowedTools` so it cannot spawn its own nested subagents.", "Blocking nested spawning on the subagent does nothing for the coordinator's missing permission or for the vague region reference."],
      ["Raise the coordinator's `max_turns` from 20 to 60 so the headless run has time to finish waiting for permission.", "More turns do not grant permission; the run is blocked on an approval no one can give, so it stalls whatever the turn limit."],
      ["Write region R-4 and the 2024–2026 window directly into the prompt the coordinator passes to the subagent.", "Subagents see only the prompt they are given, so explicit scope facts replace the dangling reference to an earlier conversation."]],
    ["water-order", "1.6", "agents", "The coordinator runs subagents sequentially: web search (180 s), then document analysis (250 s), then synthesis (70 s). Search and document analysis read different corpora and never use each other's output, but synthesis must reconcile conflicts between them using each source's publication date. End-to-end latency is about 8 minutes against a 6-minute target. How should the work be restructured?", "a", "Parallelize work that has no data dependency and serialize only where one step consumes another's output. A coordinator can emit several Agent tool calls in one response to run independent subagents concurrently, then join their results before the dependent synthesis step.",
      ["Spawn search and document analysis in parallel in one coordinator turn, then pass both dated outputs to synthesis.", "The two reads are independent, so running them together cuts latency to about 5.3 minutes, while synthesis still waits for both dated result sets."],
      ["Run all three subagents in parallel and let synthesis reconcile whatever findings have arrived by the time it starts.", "Synthesis depends on both result sets; starting it concurrently means it reconciles partial input and can miss conflicts that arrive later."],
      ["Keep the sequence but switch the document analysis subagent to a smaller, faster model to meet the target.", "A model swap may shorten one stage but leaves the unnecessary serialization in place and risks weaker analysis of the documents."],
      ["Merge search and document analysis into one subagent so a single context handles both corpora in order.", "Merging keeps the two reads serialized inside one context and removes the isolation that keeps each corpus's tools and findings apart."]],
    ["water-assert", "1.4", "agents", "Publication policy requires every report to cover all 12 regions in the brief or carry an approved exception. The report subagent's draft states 'coverage: complete', but the coordinator's structured state shows findings for 10 regions and no exception records. Last quarter, 3 of 40 published reports later proved to be missing regions. How should publication be gated?", "b", "When a business rule must always hold, enforce it in code against structured state, not against a model's own claim. A programmatic prerequisite before the irreversible step gives a deterministic guarantee; prompt rules and self-checks only make violations less frequent.",
      ["Ask the report subagent to verify its coverage claim against the source list and confirm completeness before publishing.", "The subagent already asserted completeness incorrectly; asking it to re-assess itself is self-reporting, not a control on publication."],
      ["Have the coordinator block publication in code until each of the 12 regions has findings or an approved exception.", "A programmatic gate over structured state enforces the policy whatever the draft claims, stopping the 10-of-12 report before it ships."],
      ["Publish the draft with a banner listing the two missing regions so readers can judge whether coverage is sufficient.", "Disclosure is better than silence, but policy requires coverage or an approved exception before publishing, which this skips."],
      ["Add a system prompt rule telling the report subagent never to state 'complete' unless every region has findings.", "A prompt rule lowers false claims probabilistically; the 3-in-40 failure rate shows the policy needs deterministic enforcement instead."]],
    ["water-transform", "1.5", "hooks", "Three MCP sources return publication dates as `1717200000` (Unix seconds), `\"2024-06-01T00:00:00Z\"` and `\"June 1, 2024\"`. The synthesis subagent mis-ordered 7 of 50 sources by date last week. Analysts also need the original value kept for citation audits. Which change fixes the ordering errors most reliably?", "c", "When tools return heterogeneous formats, normalize them in a PostToolUse hook so the model receives consistent data before it reasons. Keep the original value alongside the normalized one when audits need it. Prompt instructions make the model do conversion work repeatedly and unreliably.",
      ["Add a system prompt instruction telling the synthesis subagent to convert every date to ISO 8601 before comparing them.", "The model would still convert formats on every turn, so the ordering error rate falls but conversion stays probabilistic."],
      ["Add a PreToolUse hook on the three source tools that rewrites the date fields in each request to ISO 8601.", "PreToolUse sees the outgoing request, not the returned dates, so there is nothing to normalize at that point in the loop."],
      ["Add a PostToolUse hook on the three source tools that adds an ISO 8601 date field and keeps the raw original value.", "Normalizing results after the tools return and before the model reads them makes ordering deterministic, while the raw value keeps the citation audit trail."],
      ["Ask each source's maintainers to standardize on ISO 8601 and keep the current setup until all three have shipped it.", "Upstream standardization may be the long-term answer, but the ordering errors continue until three separate teams change their servers."]],
    ["water-branch", "1.7", "sessions", "An Agent SDK script captured `session_id: \"a1f3\"` after the coordinator finished a 25-minute source review. The team wants to run two synthesis strategies from that review in parallel, and an auditor will later resume `a1f3` and must see none of the experimental turns. Which SDK options should each experimental run use?", "d", "In the Agent SDK, `resume` continues a session and appends to it, while `resume` plus `fork_session` (forkSession in TypeScript) branches into a new session ID from a copy of the history. Branch when you need independent experiments from one baseline and the original must stay unchanged.",
      ["`resume=\"a1f3\"` alone, running the two strategies one after another so their turns stay in a clear order.", "Resuming without forking appends both strategies to a1f3, so the auditor would see experimental turns in the original session."],
      ["`continue_conversation=True` for both runs, which reopens the most recent session in the working directory.", "Continue picks whichever session is most recent, which may not be a1f3, and it appends to that session rather than branching."],
      ["A new session for each run, with the coordinator's final review summary pasted into the first user message.", "A fresh session leaves a1f3 clean but loses the review's tool results and reasoning, so strategies start from a summary instead."],
      ["`resume=\"a1f3\"` with `fork_session=True` for each run, so every strategy starts in a new session ID.", "Forking on resume copies the full review into a new session ID per strategy, leaving a1f3 unchanged for the auditor."]],
    ["water-tool", "1.1", "messages", "The search subagent's loop ends whenever a response contains a text block. In 18 of 200 runs it stopped after a reply like 'Let me search two more sources.' followed by two tool_use blocks, returning no findings. Each of those responses has `stop_reason: \"tool_use\"`. How should the loop decide whether to continue?", "a", "Drive an agentic loop from stop_reason. A response can contain explanatory text and tool_use blocks together; tool_use means execute the calls, append the results, and call again, while end_turn means the subagent is done. Text content, block position and iteration counts are not completion signals.",
      ["Continue while stop_reason is tool_use, running each tool and appending results, and stop when it is end_turn.", "stop_reason is the structured completion signal; a text preamble alongside tool_use blocks is normal, and the requested searches still need results."],
      ["End the loop only when the response's final content block is text, since tool_use blocks always come after preambles.", "Block position is a heuristic; a final text block can appear while more work is pending, and the rule does not read the stop signal."],
      ["Continue until the subagent's text includes a phrase such as 'research complete', then return its last message.", "Completion phrases vary between runs, so the loop either stops early or runs on; the API already provides a structured stop_reason."],
      ["Run a fixed five iterations per subagent with max_turns and return whatever findings exist after the fifth call.", "A turn cap is a safety limit; using it as the exit condition stops some runs mid-search and wastes calls on simpler ones."]],
    ["water-failure", "2.2", "mcp", "The coordinator's MCP client logs two failures. Call 1 to `search_archive_v2` returns JSON-RPC error `-32602: Unknown tool`. Call 2 to the registered `search_archive` returns a result with `isError: true` and text 'upstream index rebuilding, retry after 120 s'. The coordinator currently reports both as 'no sources found'. What distinction should it preserve?", "b", "MCP separates protocol errors, returned as JSON-RPC errors when a request is malformed or names an unknown tool, from tool execution errors, returned as results with isError: true. Execution errors belong in the model's context for recovery; protocol errors usually indicate a client or configuration bug. Neither establishes an empty result.",
      ["Both are tool execution errors that the model can fix, so pass each back to Claude and let it retry immediately.", "Call 1 never reached a tool; a protocol error for an unknown name is not something retrying the same call will fix."],
      ["Call 1 is a protocol error from a bad tool name; call 2 is a tool execution error that can be retried later.", "The unknown name failed at the JSON-RPC boundary and needs a configuration fix, while the registered tool ran and reported a recoverable outage."],
      ["Both are transport failures with no usable payload, so record both sources as unavailable and continue the report.", "Both calls returned inspectable responses; treating them as transport failures discards the retry hint and the misconfiguration signal."],
      ["Call 2 is a protocol error because the index was unavailable; call 1 is an empty search result from a new tool.", "This reverses the layers: the unknown name is the protocol error, and the rebuilding index is an execution error from a known tool."]],
    ["water-access", "2.3", "tool", "The document analysis subagent's `AgentDefinition` omits `tools`, so it inherits all 18 tools, including `web_search`, `send_email` and three admin tools. Traces show it chose a wrong tool on 12% of turns, and it ran 41 web searches last week that duplicated the search subagent's work. Its role needs 4 document tools. What is the most effective change?", "c", "Each extra tool is another option the model weighs on every turn, and out-of-role tools invite misuse. Restrict a subagent's tools list to what its role needs, and route rare outside needs through the coordinator or a narrow cross-role tool. Prompts and tool_choice do not shrink the choice set.",
      ["Add a system prompt rule restricting the subagent's scope to document work and telling it to leave web_search, send_email and admin tools unused.", "The subagent still sees 18 tools; a prompt line may lower misuse but does not remove the options causing wrong selections."],
      ["Set `tool_choice` to `{\"type\": \"any\"}` for the subagent so it must call a tool on every turn.", "Forcing a tool call controls whether a tool is used, not which one, so the 12% wrong-tool rate is untouched."],
      ["Set the AgentDefinition `tools` list to the 4 document tools, routing web lookups back through the coordinator.", "Scoping the tool list to the role removes irrelevant choices and the duplicated searches, while the coordinator keeps control of search."],
      ["Move the subagent to a larger model so it can choose more accurately among all 18 inherited tools.", "A model swap does not remove the 14 irrelevant tools, so duplicated searches and admin access both remain possible."]],
    ["water-search-edit", "2.5", "tool", "The report subagent maintains a citation library in a repository of 3,400 Markdown files. Source ID `SRC-2291` was retracted and must be replaced in every file that cites it, without altering other text. The subagent has Read, Write, Edit, Grep and Glob. Last month a full-file Write on a similar task silently dropped two footnotes. Which tool sequence fits?", "a", "Match the built-in tool to the question. Grep searches file contents, Glob matches file paths, Read loads a known file, and Edit makes a targeted string replacement. Locate with Grep, confirm context with Read, then Edit; reserve Write for new files or full rewrites.",
      ["Grep the repository contents for `SRC-2291`, Read each matching file around the hit, then apply a targeted Edit.", "Grep finds content matches across all files, Read confirms context, and Edit's old_string replacement changes only the citation."],
      ["Glob for `**/*SRC-2291*.md` to list affected files, then Write each matched file back with the new source ID.", "Glob matches file names, not contents, so citing files are missed, and full-file Write is what dropped footnotes before."],
      ["Read all 3,400 files into context in batches, note where the ID appears, then Edit each occurrence found.", "Reading every file to find a string wastes context and time when content search can locate the hits directly."],
      ["Run Edit with `replace_all: true` on a guessed index file, then Grep afterward to confirm that no references remain.", "replace_all works within one file only; guessing the file skips locating citations, and the post-check finds misses only after the edit."]],
    ["water-good-criteria", "4.1", "prompt", "The synthesis subagent merges findings it judges to be duplicates. Reviewers found 23 wrong merges in 300 reports: for example, 'EU tariff on steel, 25%, effective 2018' was merged with 'EU tariff on steel, 25%, effective 2024', erasing a renewal. The current instruction says 'combine findings that say the same thing'. Which prompt change should the team test first?", "d", "Precision comes from explicit, categorical criteria, not from confidence or caution wording. Define exactly which attributes must match for a finding to count as a duplicate, and include a near-miss example that must stay separate. Reviewers and extra effort do not supply the missing rule.",
      ["Tell the subagent to be conservative and merge two findings only when it is highly confident they are duplicates.", "Confidence language gives no rule to apply; the model was already confident these findings matched, so wrong merges continue."],
      ["Add a post-synthesis reviewer subagent that compares merged findings against their cited sources and flags suspicious merges.", "A downstream reviewer detects some wrong merges after they happen, but the merge instruction that causes them stays vague."],
      ["Increase the synthesis subagent's effort level so it reasons longer about each pair of findings before merging.", "More reasoning applied to an undefined criterion still lacks the rule that dates and sources must match for a duplicate."],
      ["Define a duplicate as matching claim, figure, jurisdiction and effective date, with a same-claim different-date example kept separate.", "A categorical criterion plus a contrasting example states exactly what counts as a duplicate, directly preventing the 2018 and 2024 merge."]],
    ["water-review", "4.6", "eval", "Each document analysis subagent reviews its own collection and passes it. After synthesis, 9 of 120 reports contain a contradiction across collections, such as a policy described as active in one section and repealed in another, with dates from different collections. Re-running each collection review with higher effort found none of the 9. Which review step best detects this failure class?", "b", "Match the review to the failure layer. Defects that exist only when outputs are combined need an integration-focused pass by an independent instance that did not produce the work. Repeating local reviews, self-rated confidence or consensus voting cannot see a contradiction that sits between collections.",
      ["Have each analysis subagent re-review and verify its collection against its sources with the first verdict visible.", "The error exists only across collections, and showing the prior verdict anchors the repeat review on its own earlier conclusion."],
      ["Run an independent integration pass over the merged report that checks claims spanning collections against both dated documents.", "An independent instance focused on the integration boundary checks exactly where the contradictions arise, without inheriting each author's reasoning."],
      ["Ask the synthesis subagent to rate its confidence in each merged claim and route low-confidence claims to a human.", "Self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated, and synthesis already produced these contradictions confidently, so they would pass the threshold."],
      ["Run the full pipeline three times and publish only claims that appear in at least two of the three reports.", "Consensus across identical runs reproduces systematic errors and can suppress correct claims that appear only intermittently."]],
    ["water-history", "5.4", "context", "A research investigation into 40 regulatory filings will span at least three context windows. In the last run, after auto-compaction the coordinator re-investigated two hypotheses it had already disproven and re-fetched 11 sources, adding 35 minutes. Verified findings and their source URLs existed only in the conversation history. What should the coordinator persist between context windows?", "c", "Long investigations need durable, structured state outside the conversation: confirmed findings with provenance, rejected hypotheses, and remaining work. Re-inject that record at each new context window. Full transcripts, final answers only, or larger windows either overfill context or drop what prevents repeated work.",
      ["The complete transcript, preserved on disk as the record of the investigation and reloaded at each new window.", "Reloading everything refills the window with the same material that forced compaction and buries the conclusions that matter."],
      ["The final answer and source list from each subagent, recorded in a running summary that the next window reads first.", "Final answers omit disproven hypotheses and pending work, so the next window can repeat dead ends and re-fetch sources."],
      ["A scratchpad file listing verified findings with source URLs, disproven hypotheses, and the filings still to check.", "A structured progress file keeps conclusions, provenance and open work compactly, so later windows resume rather than rediscover."],
      ["A larger model context window of 1M tokens so the whole investigation fits without needing compaction at all.", "A bigger window postpones compaction but forty filings can still exceed it, and long contexts dilute attention on key findings."]],
    ["water-map-gap", "5.3", "research", "A briefing is due in 10 minutes. The filings subagent covered all 6 jurisdictions. The news subagent verified jurisdictions 1–4, then failed twice on 5–6 with `429 rate_limited`; a cached news index exists but predates a ruling issued yesterday in jurisdiction 5. The briefing can act on filings data alone. What should the coordinator deliver?", "b", "Coverage is tracked per source and per scope. When part of the work fails at a deadline, deliver every verified result, mark precisely what is missing and why, and avoid filling gaps with stale substitutes or holding independent findings hostage to the failed portion.",
      ["Fill jurisdictions 5–6 from the cached news index and footnote that the cache was last refreshed before yesterday.", "The cache predates a known ruling in jurisdiction 5, so a footnote about freshness still presents outdated news as current coverage."],
      ["Deliver all filings findings and news for 1–4, marking news for 5–6 as not covered after two 429 failures.", "The briefing gets every verified result on time, and the explicit gap tells readers exactly which coverage is missing and why."],
      ["Hold the briefing until the news subagent completes 5–6, retrying with backoff until the rate limit clears.", "Filings data is independently actionable and the deadline is 10 minutes, so blocking everything on two jurisdictions delays usable findings."],
      ["Deliver only jurisdictions 1–4, where both subagents succeeded, and list 5–6 in an appendix of exclusions.", "This withholds verified filings results for 5–6 that the briefing can act on, and does not say which source is missing."]],
    ["water-source", "5.6", "grounding", "Two archived regulations share the ID `REG-118`. Version A, scanned in 2027, states a 15% cap effective 2019–2025. Version B, uploaded in 2026, states a 10% cap effective from 2026. The report must say which cap applied to a 2024 contract and which applies today, and a reviewer must be able to check each claim. Which output supports that?", "a", "Preserve claim-level provenance with the metadata that decides applicability: effective periods, publication dates, method. Sources that appear to conflict often apply to different periods. Attach the specific clause and period to each claim instead of choosing by upload order, declaring a dispute, or citing only a folder.",
      ["Report 15% for the 2024 contract and 10% today, citing each version's clause and effective period beside its claim.", "Effective periods, not scan or upload dates, decide applicability, and claim-level citations let the reviewer check each figure separately."],
      ["Report the most recently scanned version's 15% cap as current, citing version B only as a historical note.", "Scan date is not effective date; version A's period ended in 2025, so presenting 15% as current misstates today's cap."],
      ["Mark the cap as disputed and link both versions so a reviewer can decide which figure the report should use.", "The versions do not conflict once effective periods are read; flagging a dispute withholds an answer the evidence already supports."],
      ["Report both caps with their date ranges and add a link to the archive folder holding both of the scanned regulations.", "Date ranges help, but a folder link does not tie each claim to the clause that supports it, so review stays laborious."]],
  ]),
  ...caseQuestions("Code Generation with Claude Code", "Your team uses Claude Code to generate, refactor and document a TypeScript monorepo for a logistics company. The repository holds a shared scheduling contract consumed by several services, test files sit beside the code they test, and new engineers start from a clean clone. The team also runs Claude Code non-interactively in its pull-request pipeline.", [
    ["warehouse-plan-chain", "1.6", "agents", "Two tickets arrive together. T-311 renames a button label in one React component. T-312 renames `scheduleKey` to `slotId` in the shared contract; `grep` finds 230 references across 4 packages, and two consuming services live in other repositories with no documentation. Last quarter a similar rename broke a downstream service after merge. How should the work be decomposed?", "d", "Decompose by uncertainty and coupling. Well-understood local changes can run directly, while changes to a shared contract with unknown consumers need a discovery step first and a verification pass at the integration boundary after the change. Parallel package-level work and failure-driven discovery miss cross-boundary breakage.",
      ["Split the 230 references across four parallel subagents by package and merge once each package's unit tests pass.", "Package-local tests cannot reveal the undocumented external consumers, which is exactly how last quarter's rename broke after merge."],
      ["Apply both renames in one session with a single large prompt, then let integration test failures reveal the remaining consumers.", "Treating discovery as something failures will reveal repeats last quarter's incident and couples the trivial label change to a risky migration."],
      ["Plan both tickets in full first, writing a dependency map for the button label before starting either change.", "Mapping dependencies for a one-component label change adds a review cycle with no risk reduction and delays the simple fix."],
      ["Ship T-311 directly; for T-312, map consumers across repositories first, then migrate with a cross-package integration check.", "This matches effort to risk: a direct fix for the local label, and discovery plus an integration check for the shared contract."]],
    ["warehouse-resume", "1.7", "sessions", "Yesterday's Claude Code session `contract-audit` verified 18 of 25 call sites at commit `c2`. This morning an engineer ran a quick experiment in a separate session against `c3`, which is now the most recent session in the directory. The reviewer wants the remaining 7 checks appended to `contract-audit` after checking out `c2`. Which command fits?", "c", "Use `--resume <name>` to reopen a specific named session and append to it, `--continue` only when the most recent session in the directory is the one you want, and `--fork-session` when the original must stay unchanged. `-n` names a new session rather than loading an old one.",
      ["`claude --continue`, since it reopens the conversation most recently used in this directory with its full history.", "The most recent session is the c3 experiment, so --continue loads the wrong history and appends the checks there."],
      ["`claude --resume contract-audit --fork-session`, so the remaining seven checks start from the verified audit history.", "Forking loads the right history but writes into a new session ID, not the contract-audit session the reviewer asked to extend."],
      ["`claude --resume contract-audit`, reopening the named session with its tool results and appending the open checks.", "Resuming by name selects the verified c2 audit and appends new turns to that same session, as the reviewer requested."],
      ["`claude -n contract-audit`, starting a session with that name so the new checks are filed under the audit.", "-n names a new session at launch; it does not load the earlier audit's history, so the 18 verified results are absent."]],
    ["warehouse-search", "2.5", "tool", "An engineer asks Claude Code to find every test file for the scheduler, which follow the pattern `*.sched.test.ts` across 14 packages, then list which of them import the deprecated `legacyClock` helper. Claude's first attempt ran `Read` on 60 files in sequence and used 140,000 tokens. Which built-in tool approach fits the two questions?", "b", "Glob answers which files are named like a pattern; Grep answers where text appears inside files; Read loads a known file. Narrow by path with Glob, then search contents with Grep, instead of reading files one by one or concatenating them into context.",
      ["Use Grep with the pattern `*.sched.test.ts` to find the files, then Glob for `legacyClock` inside the matches.", "This swaps the tools: Glob matches path patterns and Grep searches contents, so both steps query the wrong thing."],
      ["Use Glob with `**/*.sched.test.ts` to list the files, then Grep those paths for imports of `legacyClock`.", "Glob answers the file-name question and Grep the content question, avoiding the 140,000-token cost of reading every file."],
      ["Use Bash to `cat` every TypeScript file into one stream and let Claude scan the combined output for both answers.", "Concatenating files floods context just as the sequential Reads did, and it gives no structured match list to check."],
      ["Use Read on each package's index file and infer test files and imports from what those entry points export.", "Index exports do not list test files or reveal which tests import a helper, so the answer would be incomplete."]],
    ["warehouse-team-rules", "3.1", "memory", "Engineers report that Claude names migrations correctly only on Priya's laptop. Running `/memory` there shows the naming rule in `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`; the repository's `CLAUDE.md` has no such rule. Priya's user file also says 'answer tersely, no bullet lists', which two teammates dislike. How should the instructions be reorganized?", "a", "Where a CLAUDE.md lives decides who gets it. Team conventions belong in the project file committed to the repository; personal preferences belong in `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` or a gitignored `CLAUDE.local.md`. `/memory` shows which files loaded, which tells you whether a gap is placement or wording.",
      ["Move the naming rule into the committed project `CLAUDE.md`, and leave the terse-style preference in Priya's user file.", "The team rule then reaches every clone through version control, while the personal style preference stays local to Priya."],
      ["Commit Priya's entire `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` into the repository root so every engineer gets identical behavior.", "This shares the naming rule but also imposes the terse-style preference that teammates dislike, and couples team rules to one person."],
      ["Put the naming rule in `CLAUDE.local.md` at the repository root so it applies to everyone working in the project.", "CLAUDE.local.md is a personal, gitignored file for one project, so teammates' clones would still lack the naming rule."],
      ["Ask each engineer to copy the naming rule into their own `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` and keep their style preferences there too.", "Per-person copies drift and are missing in fresh environments and CI; a team rule should travel with the repository."]],
    ["warehouse-inherit", "3.1", "memory", "The root `CLAUDE.md` says 'write tests with Jest'. Last month someone added `packages/scheduler/CLAUDE.md` saying 'use Vitest for all tests in this package'. In the scheduler package, 11 of 36 tests Claude generated this sprint used Jest, and `/context` shows both files loaded in those sessions. What is the most effective fix?", "d", "CLAUDE.md loading is additive: files from the root down and from touched subdirectories are concatenated, with no precedence between levels. When instructions conflict, Claude may follow either. Resolve contradictions in the files themselves rather than relying on emphasis, load order or per-prompt reminders.",
      ["Add 'IMPORTANT' and capital letters to the package file so its Vitest rule outranks the root file's Jest rule.", "Emphasis may shift the odds, but both instructions still load and contradict each other, so some tests will keep using Jest."],
      ["Move the Vitest rule into `CLAUDE.local.md` inside the package, which loads after and overrides project instructions.", "CLAUDE.local.md is personal and gitignored, and loading order does not create precedence; the contradiction stays for everyone else."],
      ["Delete the package file and tell engineers to mention Vitest in each prompt when they work in the scheduler.", "Per-prompt reminders are inconsistent and depend on each engineer remembering, so the 31% Jest rate would likely persist."],
      ["Reword the root rule to name Jest as the default except where a package file names another framework.", "CLAUDE.md files concatenate rather than override, so eliminating the conflicting instruction is what makes the package rule unambiguous."]],
    ["warehouse-skill", "3.2", "skills", "The team wants a `/migration-review` command that runs a 12-step checklist and accepts a migration path as its argument. It must be available to every engineer on clone or pull, and checklist changes go through code review. One engineer's personal `~/.claude/commands/migration-review.md` already exists with an older checklist. Where should the team command be created?", "b", "Project slash commands live in `.claude/commands/` (or as skills in `.claude/skills/`) in the repository, so every clone gets them and changes are reviewed. `~/.claude/commands/` is personal. A personal skill or command with the same name takes precedence for that user, so personal variants need distinct names.",
      ["In each engineer's `~/.claude/commands/migration-review.md`, distributed from a shared team wiki page whenever the checklist changes.", "Personal command files are not version-controlled with the repository, so changes skip code review and new clones get nothing."],
      ["In the repository's `.claude/commands/migration-review.md` with `$ARGUMENTS` for the path, and rename the engineer's personal copy.", "Project commands ship with the repository and go through review; renaming the personal file stops it shadowing the team command for that engineer."],
      ["In a committed `.claude/config.json` with a `commands` array entry pointing to the checklist file in the repository.", "Claude Code reads no `.claude/config.json` commands array; this configuration location does not exist, so no command would be registered."],
      ["In the root `CLAUDE.md` under a 'Migration review' heading so Claude runs the checklist whenever migrations are mentioned.", "CLAUDE.md holds standing instructions, not invocable commands, so there would be no `/migration-review` entry or argument handling."]],
    ["warehouse-slash", "3.2", "skills", "The team's `/explore-module` skill reads 30–80 files and prints its notes inline; after one run, the main session's context jumps by 90,000 tokens and later answers lose earlier decisions. Engineers also forget the required module path, and 1 in 4 invocations runs with no argument. Which `SKILL.md` frontmatter change addresses both problems?", "a", "SKILL.md frontmatter shapes how a skill runs. `context: fork` executes it in an isolated subagent so verbose exploration stays out of the main conversation and only a summary returns. `argument-hint` documents expected arguments in autocomplete. Know the real fields; distractors cite plausible ones that do not exist.",
      ["Add `context: fork` so the skill runs in a subagent that returns a summary, and `argument-hint: [module-path]`.", "Forked context keeps the file reads out of the main session, and the argument hint shows the expected path in autocomplete."],
      ["Add `allowed-tools: Read Grep` so the skill reads fewer files, and `disable-model-invocation: true` for the arguments.", "allowed-tools pre-approves tools rather than limiting how much is read, and disabling model invocation does nothing to prompt for the path."],
      ["Add `max-context: 20000` to bound the skill's token use, and `required-args: true` so it refuses to run without a path.", "Neither field exists in SKILL.md frontmatter, so the skill would keep its inline output and continue running without arguments."],
      ["Add `user-invocable: false` so only Claude decides when to explore, and `description` text that mentions the module path.", "Hiding the command from users does not move exploration out of the main context, and a description is not an argument prompt."]],
    ["warehouse-paths", "3.3", "memory", "Test files sit beside their source across 9 packages, such as `packages/route/src/plan.test.ts`. A testing convention should load whenever Claude edits any `*.test.ts` file and stay out of context for source files. The team tried a `CLAUDE.md` in each `src/` folder and found 3 of 9 copies out of date after a month. What should they commit instead?", "c", "For conventions tied to a file pattern that spans many directories, use a `.claude/rules/` file with a `paths` glob such as `**/*.test.ts`. It loads only when Claude works on matching files. Root CLAUDE.md sections load always, directory CLAUDE.md copies drift, and skills depend on invocation.",
      ["One section in the root `CLAUDE.md` headed 'Test conventions', asking Claude to apply it only when editing test files.", "Root CLAUDE.md loads in every session, so the convention fills context during source edits and relies on Claude inferring when it applies."],
      ["A `.claude/skills/testing/SKILL.md` containing the convention, with a description saying it applies to test files.", "Skills load when invoked or when Claude chooses them, so loading depends on selection rather than matching the edited file's path."],
      ["A single `.claude/rules/testing.md` file with YAML frontmatter setting `paths` to `\"**/*.test.ts\"`, written once for all packages.", "A path-scoped rule loads only when Claude touches a matching file anywhere in the tree, with one copy to maintain instead of nine."],
      ["A `.claude/config.json` entry mapping the glob `**/*.test.ts` to a conventions file stored under `docs/testing.md`.", "Claude Code has no `.claude/config.json` mapping; path-conditional instructions are configured through `paths` frontmatter in `.claude/rules/` files."]],
    ["warehouse-path-test", "3.3", "memory", "A new rule, `.claude/rules/migrations.md`, should load only for files under `db/migrations/`. Engineers notice its text in sessions that never touch migrations, and `/context` shows it loaded at session start. Its frontmatter reads `paths: db/migrations/**/*.sql, \"db/seeds/*.sql`. Four other rules with `paths` frontmatter load correctly. What is the most likely cause?", "b", "Path-scoped rules depend on valid YAML frontmatter. If the `paths` block fails to parse, the rule loads as though it had no `paths`, which means always. Globs resolve relative to the project root and may be listed. When a scoped rule appears everywhere, check the frontmatter syntax first, for example with `claude --debug`.",
      ["Rules in `.claude/rules/` load at launch regardless of `paths`, which only changes the order rules appear in context.", "Valid `paths` frontmatter does make a rule conditional; the four other rules loading correctly show path scoping works in this project."],
      ["The YAML fails to parse because of the unclosed quote, so Claude Code treats the rule as having no `paths` at all.", "A rule whose frontmatter cannot be parsed loads as if unscoped, which means every session; fixing the YAML restores path matching."],
      ["The glob resolves relative to the rule file's folder, so `db/migrations/**` matches nothing and the rule falls back to global loading.", "Patterns resolve relative to the project root, and a pattern that matched nothing would keep the rule unloaded, not load it everywhere."],
      ["`paths` only accepts a single glob string, so listing two patterns makes Claude Code apply the rule to every file.", "`paths` accepts a list of patterns; the problem is the malformed quoting, not the number of globs in the field."]],
    ["warehouse-modes", "3.4", "cli", "An engineer must replace the in-house job queue with a managed queue service. The change touches an estimated 45 files in 6 packages, and two designs are viable: an adapter layer or a direct rewrite of every producer. Nothing should be edited until the team approves an approach. Which way of starting Claude Code fits?", "d", "Plan mode is a permission mode in which Claude reads, explores and proposes, but cannot edit until you approve. Use it when a change is large, architectural or has several viable designs; enter it with Shift+Tab, `/plan`, or `claude --permission-mode plan`. Use direct execution for small, well-scoped fixes.",
      ["Start in direct execution with detailed upfront instructions for the adapter design, and review the diff afterward.", "This picks a design before exploring the code and reviews only after 45 files have changed, missing the approval gate."],
      ["Start in direct execution on one package and switch to plan mode only if unexpected complexity appears there.", "The complexity is already stated in the requirements; waiting for it to emerge means edits happen before an approach is approved."],
      ["Launch with `claude --mode readonly` so Claude can explore the queue code but is blocked from writing files.", "There is no `--mode readonly` flag; read-only planning is the plan permission mode, started with `--permission-mode plan`."],
      ["Launch with `claude --permission-mode plan` so Claude explores both designs and proposes a plan before any edits.", "Plan mode lets Claude read and propose while edits stay blocked until approval, which fits a 45-file change with two viable designs."]],
    ["warehouse-mode-safety", "3.4", "cli", "In plan mode, Claude proposed renaming `slot.start` to `slot.startsAt` across 22 files. Its final read-only search then found `night-runner/export.ts`, deployed separately every Sunday, reading `slot.start` directly. Policy requires an approved plan before edits, and the night runner cannot ship a change for 9 days. What should happen next?", "a", "A plan is only as good as the dependencies it accounts for. When plan-mode exploration reveals an undocumented consumer, revise the plan for compatibility and get it re-approved before editing. Narrowing edit scope, editing first, or adding detective tests do not protect a separately deployed reader.",
      ["Stay in plan mode, revise the plan to keep `slot.start` readable until the runner ships, and get it approved.", "The new consumer changes the migration design, so revising for backward compatibility and re-approving keeps edits behind the policy gate."],
      ["Approve the current plan and exclude `night-runner/export.ts` from the edit scope so it keeps working unchanged.", "Leaving the runner unedited does not help: once 22 files rename the field, the runner reads a property that no longer exists."],
      ["Switch to accept-edits mode, implement dual reads of both fields, then send the revised plan for approval afterward.", "Dual reads may be the right design, but implementing them before approval violates the policy that plans are approved first."],
      ["Approve the plan as written and add a CI test that fails if any file still references `slot.start`.", "That test would fail on the runner's legitimate reference, and it detects breakage rather than designing compatibility for the 9-day gap."]],
    ["warehouse-feedback", "3.5", "prompt", "Claude's fix to `parseWindow()` made the 4 failing tests for missing arrays pass, but CI now shows 3 new failures: `parseWindow([])` returns `null` instead of `[]`, and `parseWindow(undefined)` throws. The engineer's next message, drafted so far, reads 'still broken, please make it more robust'. What should the engineer send instead?", "c", "When iterating with Claude, concrete examples beat adjectives. Give the exact failing inputs with their expected outputs, including neighboring cases that must not change, and ask for the tests to be rerun. Vague requests, raw logs and fresh rewrites leave the intended contract unstated.",
      ["A request to handle all falsy inputs the same way so null, undefined and empty arrays share one code path.", "Collapsing inputs that need different outputs reintroduces the bug; an empty array must stay `[]` while missing input has its own result."],
      ["The full CI log of 400 lines with a request to make every test pass without changing the test files.", "The log hides the three specific cases in noise and states a goal without the input-to-output contract the fix must satisfy."],
      ["The three inputs with expected outputs, `[]` for `[]` and the agreed results for `null` and `undefined`, plus a rerun request.", "Concrete input-output pairs define the boundary precisely and make the next attempt checkable against the same cases that failed."],
      ["A request to rewrite `parseWindow()` from scratch using a different approach, since the incremental fix regressed.", "A rewrite discards the part that now works and still lacks the expected behaviors that caused the regression."]],
    ["warehouse-match", "3.5", "prompt", "In the replay importer, the key `shift/05/late` becomes `shift/05` after deserialization, while the live importer keeps `shift/05/late`; both call `decodeKey()`. Seventeen downstream tests fail. Claude's previous attempt edited 9 test assertions and left the defect in place. Which correction brief best helps Claude localize the fault and protect the working path?", "b", "Effective correction briefs locate the fault and define success: give the failing input with observed and expected output, a contrasting passing case that shares the code path, and constraints that block shortcuts such as editing tests. That lets Claude find the first divergence and confirm it did not break the working path.",
      ["List the 17 failing tests and ask Claude to make them pass, reviewing upstream code only if assertions are correct.", "Leading with downstream symptoms repeats the last attempt's pattern of editing assertions instead of finding the first wrong transformation."],
      ["Give the replay input, observed and expected keys, the passing live case, and forbid edits to test assertions.", "The contrasting live case points to replay-specific handling, exact outputs make the fix testable, and the constraint blocks the previous shortcut."],
      ["Ask Claude to rewrite `decodeKey()` so both importers share one stricter parser, then rerun the full suite.", "decodeKey is shared and works for live input; prescribing a rewrite there risks breaking the working path before locating the replay difference."],
      ["Give the replay input and expected key, and limit Claude to the replay importer and its single fixture test.", "Well scoped, but it omits the passing live control, so a change that breaks the live path would go unnoticed."]],
    ["warehouse-ci-mode", "3.6", "cli", "The PR review job runs `claude -p \"Review this diff\" --output-format text`, and a regex extracts findings from the Markdown. Last week 14 of 300 runs posted malformed comments when the model changed its heading style. The poster needs `file`, `line`, `severity` and `message` per finding, and the job must not modify the checkout. Which invocation fits?", "d", "For CI, run `claude -p` so nothing waits for input, and use `--output-format json` with `--json-schema` to get a validated answer in `structured_output`; check the field exists before posting. Pre-approve only the read tools the job needs. Prompted JSON and stream parsing are not schema-validated.",
      ["Keep `--output-format text` and add a system prompt demanding strict JSON with the four fields and no Markdown.", "Prompted JSON usually parses but is not validated against a schema, so format drift still reaches the poster occasionally."],
      ["Use `--output-format stream-json` and parse the final streamed event's text as the findings list for posting.", "stream-json streams events for real-time consumers; the final text is still unvalidated prose, so heading changes still break parsing."],
      ["Set `CLAUDE_OUTPUT=json` in the job environment so Claude Code emits machine-readable findings in headless runs.", "No such environment variable exists in Claude Code; output format is set with `--output-format`, and schemas with `--json-schema`."],
      ["Add `--output-format json --json-schema` with the four fields, read `structured_output`, and pass read-only `--allowedTools`.", "The schema-validated `structured_output` gives typed findings whatever the wording, and pre-approving only read tools keeps the review read-only."]],
    ["warehouse-ci-id", "3.6", "eval", "The PR review job reruns on every push. On a PR with 5 pushes, it posted the same 'unchecked null in `assignSlot`' finding 5 times, and developers resolved 31 duplicate comments last week. The job does not pass earlier findings to Claude, and the poster does not deduplicate. Which change best reduces duplicates without hiding new issues?", "c", "Repeated CI runs need two things to avoid duplicate comments: the model must see prior findings so it reports only new or still-unresolved issues, and the publisher must be idempotent, keyed on a stable finding identity. Skipping runs or raising thresholds trades duplicates for missed defects.",
      ["Run the review only on the first push of each PR, and skip reruns until a reviewer requests another pass.", "Skipping later pushes removes duplicates by also missing any new defects introduced after the first push."],
      ["Add a prompt instruction telling Claude to compare against earlier pushes and not repeat findings already recorded.", "Claude cannot know what earlier runs reported unless those findings are supplied, so the instruction has nothing to compare against."],
      ["Include prior findings in the prompt and have the poster update comments keyed by file, line and rule.", "Supplying prior findings lets Claude report only new or changed issues, and stable keys make posting idempotent across reruns."],
      ["Raise the severity threshold so only critical findings are posted, which lowers how many comments each rerun adds.", "A higher threshold reduces volume across the board, suppressing legitimate lower-severity issues while duplicates of critical findings continue."]],
  ]),
  ...caseQuestions("Structured Data Extraction", "You are building a structured data extraction system for a freight broker. Claude extracts fields from scanned bills of lading and delivery receipts, some handwritten, through an extraction tool whose `input_schema` defines the output. Results feed a billing system that rejects malformed records. Reviewers can check 120 records a day, and original documents must be retained unchanged.", [
    ["cold-handoff", "1.3", "subagents", "Two extraction subagents process a 40-page shipment packet in parallel: one handles pages 1–20, the other 21–40. Each currently returns a prose paragraph. Last week the coordinator matched 6 of 52 line items to the wrong shipment; both halves mention shipment numbers `SH-4410` and `SH-4401`. What should each subagent return?", "d", "Subagent outputs should be structured for the coordinator that consumes them: stable identifiers, values, provenance such as page or document, and explicit unresolved items. That lets parallel results merge deterministically. Prose summaries, raw logs and single scores force the coordinator to infer relationships it should be given.",
      ["A longer prose summary that repeats the shipment number in every sentence so the coordinator can follow it.", "More repetition makes mistakes rarer but leaves the coordinator parsing prose to recover item-to-shipment links, which is where errors occur."],
      ["Its full tool-call log in order, so the coordinator can replay the extraction and work out which page held each item.", "Raw logs push the reconstruction work onto the coordinator and still rely on inference to link items to shipments."],
      ["A single confidence score for the packet half, with low scores triggering a full re-extraction by the coordinator.", "One score per half says nothing about which items belong to which shipment, and self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated."],
      ["Structured records per line item with shipment ID, page number, extracted values and any fields left unresolved.", "Keyed, structured records let the coordinator merge halves by shipment ID and page without interpreting prose, and show what remains open."]],
    ["cold-tool-scope", "2.1", "tool", "The extraction subagent has two tools: `get_document(doc_id)`, described as 'Gets a delivery document', and `post_receipt(doc_id)`, described as 'Gets a delivery document and records it as received'. In 2,000 extractions last week, it called `post_receipt` 37 times, marking undelivered freight as received. Its role never requires recording receipts. What is the most effective first change?", "a", "When an agent misuses a tool its role never needs, remove the tool from that agent rather than trying to steer selection. Description and example fixes are right when both tools are legitimate choices; compensating hooks act after the side effect has already happened.",
      ["Remove `post_receipt` from the extraction subagent's tools, leaving receipt posting to the separately gated billing workflow.", "The subagent has no legitimate need for the write tool, so removing it eliminates the 37 erroneous receipts at their source."],
      ["Rewrite both descriptions to explain that `post_receipt` has a side effect, keeping both tools available to the extraction subagent.", "Clearer descriptions would lower misselection, but a role that never records receipts keeps write capability it does not need."],
      ["Add few-shot examples to the extraction prompt showing `get_document` chosen for every read-only extraction request.", "Examples make the right choice more likely but leave the write tool reachable, so some erroneous receipts would continue."],
      ["Add a PostToolUse hook that reverses any receipt posted during extraction and alerts the operations channel.", "PostToolUse runs after the receipt is already recorded; reversing it afterward is compensation, not prevention, and billing may act first."]],
    ["cold-tool-data", "2.2", "mcp", "`fetch_attachments(shipment_id: \"SH-77\")` returns 10 attachments; the result has `isError: false`, but 2 entries carry `status: \"unavailable\", reason: \"storage timeout\"` with no content. The extraction subagent wrote a record listing 8 attachments and no gaps, and billing approved the shipment. How should the tool result and the subagent handle partial retrieval?", "b", "A partial failure is neither success nor total failure. Return the data that was retrieved, mark exactly which items failed and why, and carry that unresolved state into the output so downstream systems do not treat incomplete evidence as complete. Silent drops and invented values hide the gap.",
      ["Set `isError: true` on the whole result whenever any attachment fails, so the subagent discards all 10 and retries.", "Failing the entire call throws away eight good attachments and turns a partial gap into a total failure that blocks extraction."],
      ["Keep the 8 attachments as data, and record the 2 unavailable ones as unresolved with their reason in the output.", "Partial results stay usable while the explicit gap prevents billing from treating the shipment as completely documented."],
      ["Drop the two failed entries in a PostToolUse hook so the subagent records a clean, validated list of eight attachments.", "Hiding the failed entries is exactly what let billing approve an incomplete shipment; the gap must reach the output, not vanish."],
      ["Have the subagent infer the contents of the two missing attachments from the shipment's other documents and fill them in.", "Inferred contents are fabricated evidence; storage timeouts mean the documents exist but were not read, which is an unresolved state."]],
    ["cold-review-criteria", "4.1", "prompt", "A review step checks extracted weights against the bill of lading. The contract tolerance is ±0.5% of declared weight. Over 400 shipments it flagged 61, of which operations dismissed 52 as within tolerance (for example 1,002 kg against 1,000 kg), and it missed a 2.1% discrepancy. The prompt says 'flag weights that look inconsistent'. What prompt change should be tested first?", "c", "Vague review criteria produce false positives and misses because the model substitutes its own notion of what matters. Replace adjectives with a categorical, testable rule tied to the documented threshold, and illustrate both sides of the boundary. Confidence filters and larger models do not supply the missing rule.",
      ["Tell the reviewer to be conservative and flag a weight only when it is highly confident the difference matters.", "Confidence wording gives no numeric rule, so the reviewer still guesses what 'matters' and may suppress the 2.1% case too."],
      ["Have the reviewer attach a 1–10 confidence score and auto-dismiss flags scored below 7 before operations sees them.", "Self-rated confidence is not calibrated to the contract tolerance, so dismissals would not track the ±0.5% boundary."],
      ["State the rule as flag when the difference exceeds 0.5% of declared weight, with examples at 0.2% and 2.1%.", "A categorical numeric criterion with examples on both sides tells the reviewer exactly where the boundary is."],
      ["Switch the review step to a larger model that is better at comparing and flagging weight differences that look inconsistent.", "A stronger model still applies the same undefined criterion, so false flags and misses depend on its guess about inconsistency."]],
    ["cold-examples", "4.2", "prompt", "Of 300 extraction errors reviewed, 212 confuse two cases: a weight box with a smudged, unreadable entry and a weight box left blank. Both should produce `weight_kg: null`, but `weight_status` must be `\"unreadable\"` or `\"missing\"` respectively. The prompt has room for two more examples. Which two examples teach this boundary?", "ac", "Few-shot examples teach boundaries best as minimal contrasting pairs: two inputs that differ only in the feature the model confuses, each with its exact expected output. Spend example slots on the observed failure, not on cases already handled or unrelated variety.",
      ["A receipt with a visible but smudged weight entry, labeled `weight_kg: null` and `weight_status: \"unreadable\"`.", "This shows the present-but-illegible side of the boundary with the exact target output for both fields."],
      ["A receipt with a clearly printed weight of 1,240 kg, labeled with the parsed value and a `\"present\"` status.", "Clear printed weights are already handled correctly, so this example spends a slot on a case that is not failing."],
      ["A receipt whose weight box is empty, labeled `weight_kg: null` and `weight_status: \"missing\"` for the extraction.", "Paired with the smudged case, this shows the same null value with a different status, isolating exactly what distinguishes them."],
      ["A receipt with a handwritten weight in pounds, labeled with the converted kilogram value and a conversion note.", "Unit conversion is a different difficulty; it adds variety without teaching the unreadable-versus-missing distinction behind 212 errors."],
      ["A receipt with both a smudged weight and a blank date, labeled only with a free-text note describing the problems.", "Without the structured target output, the example does not show which status value each situation should produce."]],
    ["cold-edge-examples", "4.2", "prompt", "The extractor handles printed zeros and blank boxes correctly. On 23 receipts, a driver wrote 'TBD – pending reweigh' in the charge box, and Claude output `charge_usd: 0`. Billing treats 0 as a confirmed no-charge shipment, while `null` with `charge_status: \"pending\"` holds the invoice. There is room for one more example. Which example targets this failure?", "d", "When a model mishandles one specific input pattern, the most valuable example shows that exact pattern with the exact target output, distinct from neighboring cases already handled. Examples that cover nearby cases or attach a flag to the wrong value leave the failing boundary untaught.",
      ["A receipt with a handwritten '0' in the charge box, labeled `charge_usd: 0` and `charge_status: \"confirmed\"`.", "This adds handwriting coverage for a genuine zero, which already extracts correctly, and leaves the pending notation unlabeled."],
      ["A receipt with an empty charge box, labeled `charge_usd: null` and `charge_status: \"missing\"` for billing.", "Blank boxes are already handled; absence is a different state from a written note saying the charge is pending."],
      ["A receipt marked 'pending reweigh', labeled `charge_usd: 0` with a `needs_review: true` flag added for the billing team.", "The flag records doubt but still sends 0, which billing reads as a confirmed no-charge shipment, so invoices are released wrongly."],
      ["A receipt marked 'TBD – pending reweigh', labeled `charge_usd: null` and `charge_status: \"pending\"` as the target.", "It demonstrates the exact notation that fails with the output billing needs, teaching that pending text is neither zero nor missing."]],
    ["cold-shape", "4.3", "structured", "The extractor sends an `extract_bol` tool with an `input_schema` and `tool_choice: {\"type\": \"auto\"}`. In 4% of 10,000 requests, Claude replies in text ('The weight appears to be about 1,200 kg') instead of calling the tool, and the parser fails. In another 1%, `weight_kg` is the string `\"unknown\"`. Which change addresses both failures?", "c", "To guarantee structured output through tool use, set `tool_choice` to a named tool, or to `any` when several schemas are offered, and make the schema itself express allowed types, using nullable fields for possibly missing values. Prompt rules and retries reduce failures; schema and tool_choice prevent them.",
      ["Keep `tool_choice` on auto and add a system prompt rule that every reply must call `extract_bol` with numeric weights.", "A prompt rule lowers the 4% text-reply rate but does not guarantee a tool call, and it cannot constrain the field type."],
      ["Set `tool_choice: {\"type\": \"none\"}` and ask Claude to return the schema as JSON text that a regex then validates.", "`none` forbids tool use entirely, so every response becomes unconstrained text and parsing failures increase rather than disappear."],
      ["Force the tool with `tool_choice: {\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"extract_bol\"}` and type `weight_kg` as number or null with strict schema.", "A forced tool guarantees a tool_use block every time, and a strict number-or-null type stops strings like 'unknown' at generation."],
      ["Set `tool_choice: {\"type\": \"any\"}` and add a retry loop that re-sends any response whose weight is not numeric.", "`any` would stop text replies here, but the schema still allows strings, so retries treat the type problem after the fact."]],
    ["cold-output-truth", "4.3", "structured", "The schema restricts `shipment_id` to an enum of 140 active shipments, and every response validates. An audit of 500 records found 9 where Claude returned a valid but wrong ID, for example `SH-4401` for a receipt stamped `SH-4410`, and billing charged the wrong customer. What should the pipeline add?", "a", "Schemas guarantee shape and allowed values, not truth. When several valid values are possible, add a semantic validation step that compares the output with an independent source, such as the document text or the originating request, and route mismatches to review. Plausibility ratings and citation fields do not verify the value.",
      ["A semantic check comparing the returned ID with the receipt's OCR stamp and the request, routing mismatches to review.", "The schema already guarantees an active ID; only a comparison against the document and request catches a valid but wrong value."],
      ["A stricter schema validating `shipment_id` against the 140 active IDs with a regex pattern that matches the stamp format.", "Both the wrong and the right ID are active, so any schema-level restriction still accepts SH-4401 for this receipt."],
      ["A second model call that rates whether the extracted shipment ID looks plausible, rejecting answers rated below 0.8.", "Plausibility ratings cannot tell two valid IDs apart without checking the source stamp, so transposed digits still pass."],
      ["A required `citation` field in the schema so each extraction records and documents where the shipment ID was found.", "A citation field only proves a string was produced; nothing verifies that the cited text actually contains the returned ID."]],
    ["cold-repair-date", "4.4", "structured", "A carrier's receipts print dates as `08/03/2026`; the carrier confirmed in writing that its format is DD/MM/YYYY. The extractor returns the string verbatim, and billing rejects 340 records a week because it accepts only ISO 8601. Last month a retry with 'output ISO dates' produced `2026-08-03` for 41 records, swapping month and day. Which fix should the pipeline apply?", "d", "Separate representation problems from factual ones. When the source value is settled and its format is known, fix the representation with deterministic code and validate the result. Re-asking the model, voting across runs, or pattern-only checks risk changing the fact itself, as a day-month swap shows.",
      ["Retry extraction with a stricter prompt and an ISO date pattern in the schema, accepting any date that matches it.", "A pattern checks shape only; `2026-08-03` matches perfectly yet swaps day and month, which is the exact error seen before."],
      ["Have Claude extract each date twice and accept the ISO value when both runs agree on the same result.", "Two runs can share the same systematic month-day swap, and agreement says nothing about which interpretation is correct."],
      ["Preserve the original `08/03/2026` string unchanged in the date field and record the carrier's confirmed format beside it.", "Keeping the source string preserves the fact but violates the billing contract, so the 340 weekly rejections continue."],
      ["Parse the extracted string in code with the confirmed DD/MM/YYYY format, emit `2026-03-08`, and validate before posting.", "The format is known, so deterministic conversion fixes representation without asking the model to reinterpret a settled fact."]],
    ["cold-conflict", "4.4", "structured", "A bill of lading and a delivery receipt for shipment `SH-5120` both pass schema validation but list seal numbers `K104` and `K140`. Neither document is designated authoritative. Intake may proceed with either seal recorded, but automatic release to the consignee requires one verified seal. Last quarter 3 cargo claims traced back to auto-resolved seal conflicts. How should the pipeline proceed?", "b", "Schema validation cannot resolve conflicting facts. When sources disagree and no precedence rule exists, keep both claims with attribution, let any step that does not depend on the answer proceed, and block the step that does until a human resolves it. Do not invent precedence or force agreement.",
      ["Select `K104` because the bill of lading is created earlier in the shipping process and is usually more accurate.", "A general belief about document order is not a designated precedence rule, and auto-resolving seal conflicts caused last quarter's claims."],
      ["Record intake with both seal claims and their source documents, block automatic release, and route the conflict to staff.", "Intake proceeds as permitted, both claims stay attributed, and release waits for a human to check the physical seal."],
      ["Hold the whole shipment, including intake, until staff confirm and document the seal, then store only the verified value.", "Blocking release is right, but intake is explicitly permitted, and storing only the adjudicated value discards the original conflicting evidence."],
      ["Ask Claude to re-extract and compare both documents with higher effort, and release when the seals then match.", "Both documents may genuinely disagree; re-extraction cannot inspect the physical seal and may force agreement that hides a real discrepancy."]],
    ["cold-deadline", "4.5", "batch", "Dispatchers need a carrier-rate extraction within 3 seconds while a customer waits on the phone. Separately, 18,000 archived receipts must be re-extracted before an audit in 4 days; today 30% of those runs call a `lookup_carrier` tool mid-extraction. Finance proposes moving both workloads from synchronous calls to Message Batches for the 50% discount. Which plan fits?", "a", "Message Batches cut cost by 50% but complete within 24 hours with no latency guarantee, and each request runs as a single call, so your code cannot answer tool calls mid-request. Use batches for latency-tolerant, self-contained work keyed by custom_id, and keep interactive or tool-looping work synchronous.",
      ["Keep dispatch synchronous; batch the archive as single-turn requests with carrier data inline, keyed by `custom_id`.", "Batches suit the 4-day archive but not the 3-second path, and each batched request is one call, so tool lookups must be removed first."],
      ["Batch both workloads and poll the dispatch batch every second, falling back to a synchronous call after 3 seconds.", "Batches can take up to 24 hours, so dispatch would nearly always hit the fallback, adding complexity for no saving."],
      ["Batch the archive unchanged, letting the harness answer each `lookup_carrier` call once the batch results arrive later.", "A batched request cannot pause for your code to return a tool result, so the 30% needing lookups would end unfinished."],
      ["Keep both synchronous, because batch results return in arbitrary order and cannot be matched to their receipts.", "Batch results are matched by `custom_id`, so ordering is not a blocker; this forgoes the discount on latency-tolerant work."]],
    ["cold-results", "4.5", "batch", "An overnight batch of 6,000 extractions ends with `processing_status: \"ended\"`. Results show 5,912 `succeeded`, 61 `errored` (52 `overloaded_error`, 9 `invalid_request_error` for documents over the context limit) and 27 `expired`. Results arrive in a different order from submission. What is the most efficient recovery?", "d", "Match batch results by custom_id, never by position. Keep succeeded results, then handle failures by cause: resubmit transient errors and expired requests as they are, and fix invalid requests, for example by chunking oversized documents, before resubmitting. Rerunning everything or resending invalid requests unchanged wastes cost.",
      ["Resubmit all 6,000 requests unchanged in a new batch and overwrite stored results by submission position.", "Rerunning succeeded work wastes cost, and positional matching attaches results to the wrong receipts because order is not preserved."],
      ["Store succeeded results by position, then resubmit all 88 failed and expired requests unchanged in one batch.", "Position is unreliable for out-of-order results, and resubmitting the 9 oversized documents unchanged will fail the same way again."],
      ["Store succeeded results by `custom_id`, then send the 88 remaining records through synchronous calls tonight.", "custom_id matching is right, but moving 88 non-urgent requests to full-price synchronous calls is unnecessary, and the 9 oversized documents still fail."],
      ["Store succeeded results by `custom_id`, resubmit the 79 overloaded and expired as-is, and chunk the 9 oversized documents first.", "This keeps completed work, retries transient failures cheaply in a new batch, and fixes the cause before resubmitting the invalid requests."]],
    ["cold-second-pass", "4.6", "eval", "Version 2 of the extractor agreed with version 1 on 97% of handwritten weights, so the team called it validated. A labeled audit then found both versions misread the same 31 of 400 handwritten '7's as '1's. The team wants a review step that catches this kind of shared error before records reach billing. Which design fits?", "b", "Agreement between two runs or versions of the same system is not independent verification; shared systematic errors pass unnoticed. Use a reviewer that did not generate the output, examining the source evidence, and measure it against labeled cases. Self-reported confidence and disagreement-only review miss confident, shared mistakes.",
      ["Run version 2 again on each handwritten record and accept a weight when both runs match and pass schema validation.", "Repeat runs share the same systematic misreading, so agreement would accept the 31 errors just as the version comparison did."],
      ["Have an independent review instance read the handwritten weight image afresh, scored against the labeled audit set.", "A reviewer that did not produce the extraction, measured against labels, can catch errors both versions share instead of repeating them."],
      ["Ask the extractor to report its confidence per weight and route values below 0.7 to the review queue for verification.", "The shared misreads were produced confidently, so a self-reported threshold would leave most of the 31 errors unreviewed."],
      ["Compare versions 1 and 2 on every record and send only their disagreements to human reviewers before billing.", "Disagreement review misses exactly this failure: the 31 errors are cases where both versions agreed on the same wrong value."]],
    ["cold-coverage", "5.5", "eval", "Records with extractor confidence above 0.9 post automatically. A labeled audit found 18 wrong amounts among 60 high-confidence handwritten receipts and 1 among 240 printed ones. About 70 handwritten receipts arrive daily, and reviewers can check 120 records a day. Which routing policy should the team adopt first?", "a", "Calibrate review routing with labeled outcomes broken down by slice, not aggregate confidence. Send the slice with evidenced errors to review when capacity allows, and keep a stratified sample of auto-accepted work so new failure modes stay visible. Global thresholds, uniform sampling and complaints miss concentrated risk.",
      ["Route all handwritten amounts to review and spend the remaining 50 slots on a stratified sample of auto-posted printed records.", "The 70 daily handwritten receipts fit capacity and carry the evidenced 30% error rate, while sampling keeps watch for other failures."],
      ["Raise the auto-post threshold to 0.97 for all records and review whatever falls below it, up to 120 a day.", "The errors occurred above 0.9 confidence, so a higher global cutoff may still pass them while flooding review with printed records."],
      ["Spend all 120 slots on a uniform random sample across both document types and auto-post everything else.", "Random sampling supports monitoring but leaves most of a known high-error slice auto-posting, about 21 wrong amounts a day."],
      ["Keep the current threshold and use customer billing complaints to identify which receipts need re-review.", "Complaints arrive after wrong amounts are billed, and complaint rates need not match where errors actually occur."]],
    ["cold-keyed-state", "5.1", "context", "A long extraction session handles shipments for one shipper: pallet A and pallet B on run `R-17`, then pallet A and pallet B again on run `R-18`. After compaction, the summary read 'pallet A: 4.1 °C, pallet B: 7.9 °C', and the session posted R-17's temperatures onto R-18's records. What state should the session carry forward?", "d", "Durable case facts need keys that are unique across the whole session. When identifiers such as pallet letters repeat across runs, use a composite key so compaction and handoffs cannot reassign values. Structured, keyed records survive summarization; prose order and letter-only keys do not.",
      ["Each verified temperature keyed by pallet letter with its source, keeping the newest reading whenever a pallet letter repeats.", "Pallet letters repeat across runs, so keying by letter alone lets R-18 overwrite R-17, which is the collision that caused the error."],
      ["A prose summary that records both runs in the order they were processed, matching temperatures in reading order.", "Order-dependent prose is the same structure compaction already scrambled; the pallet-to-temperature link still depends on narrative position."],
      ["The full uncompacted transcript, preserved and reloaded so the session can re-derive which temperature matches which run.", "Reloading everything refills context that forced compaction and still asks the model to reconstruct associations by reading."],
      ["A structured entry keyed by run and pallet, holding each temperature with its unit, source page and verified status.", "A composite run-plus-pallet key keeps every measurement attached to the right record through compaction and handoff."]],
  ]),
];
